  "id": "pop-trades",
  "owner": "W1-17 round 2. The three trade registers the dialogue was written for and the population never supplied.",
  "source": "corpus/90-verdicts/wave1/W1-17-r1.md §C2/§C3 and §J (GAP-W1-17-an-archetype-with-no-mouth).",
  "hand_authored": true,
  "do_not_regenerate": true,
  "why_this_file_exists": [
    "The round-1 critic measured 133 of 1,280 authored infos (10.4%) as hearable by no player from any speaker.",
    "Ninety of those are gated to an (actor, town) pair no such actor stands in, and forty name an actor no NPC in the province is.",
    "The cause is a population/dialogue ratio that is out of line with every other register in the build. Measured over the shipped tree:",
    "  archivist   124 authored infos /  2 bodies = 62.0 infos per speaker",
    "  sapcutter   116 authored infos /  4 bodies = 29.0",
    "  mudborn     112 authored infos / 12 bodies =  9.3",
    "  ...against rootkeeper 183/30 = 6.1, legionary 118/31 = 3.8, clerk 97/20 = 4.9, townsman 93/72 = 1.3.",
    "So this is not a writing defect and it is not a metric artefact: the province was written as though it had records-keepers and travelling sap-buyers in every town, and the population files gave them to two towns each.",
    "speakers.json's own definition of the sapcutter says so outright — 'the Sap-Cutters' ITINERANT buyers, and everyone else working a cart BETWEEN TOWNS'. An itinerant trade that exists in two of eight settlements is a contradiction inside the data, not a shortfall to be linted away.",
    "These records are therefore BODIES for lines that already exist. Not one word of dialogue was written for this file, and no info's text was changed to suit it."
  ],
  "the_magister_decision": [
    "RI-DLG06 §B row 2 requires the Telvanni-analogue archetype and calls it `magister`. Twenty-three infos are authored for it, tools/dialogue/voice-metrics.mjs measures its register, and game/data/dialogue/speakers.json sets its bands and its address form ('provincial'). No NPC in the province carried actor `magister`, so every one of those lines was silent.",
    "The archetype STAYS, and the argument is that the register was never mouthless — only the office was. speakers.json defines the magister's `who` as 'House Dres magisters AND FACTORS', and the province already has 25 `dres-factor` speakers carrying 31 infos in exactly that register. What was missing was the singular officer the writing keeps naming.",
    "The corpus states the office is singular and says how many there are: `house-dres#3` — 'Six estates, four factors, one magister and a treaty that says none of it is ours.' A topic `magister-varo-dren` answers 'I am he, if you were hoping for someone else', and Varo Dren was not in the roster at all.",
    "So exactly ONE magister is added, in the capital, and that is the whole of it. Adding a second would contradict the line that motivates the first.",
    "The alternative — deleting the archetype and its voice bands — was rejected because it would delete 23 lines of the best-voiced writing in the corpus to make a lint pass, and because RI-DLG06 §B requires six archetypes and would then have five."
  ],
  "placement_note": "Every record names a settlement AND an interior that game/data/world/settlements/<town>.json actually declares. That is load-bearing: Engine.populateSettlement() derives a position from the interior's bounds, and a record with a settlement but no interior and no post.pos falls through to the else-branch and is placed near the world origin, kilometres from the town it claims — the defect that branch's own comment calls 'defect 2'. A body that is not in the town is not a body.",
  "index_note": "game/data/index.json carries an entry for this path. The engine builds this.data.npcs from index.json, NOT from a directory scan, so a population file that is not indexed is visible to every tool in tools/dialogue/ and invisible to the running game — which is precisely the shape of defect this round exists to remove.",
